WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.581
This is one ticket in Symphony, from the board to Done.

s1.2
00:00:03.581 --> 00:00:08.011
A person writes the ticket in Linear, or as an issue on a GitHub Projects board.

s1.3
00:00:08.011 --> 00:00:11.534
The daemon picks it up, and a runner starts one coding agent for it.

s1.4
00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:15.757
The agent works in its own workspace pod and opens a ready pull request.

s1.5
00:00:15.757 --> 00:00:18.499
The merger reviews the pull request and merges it.

s1.6
00:00:18.499 --> 00:00:22.036
After Done, a reflector looks back and proposes lessons.

s2.1
00:00:22.386 --> 00:00:25.729
The daemon dispatches a ticket only when every rule holds.

s2.2
00:00:25.729 --> 00:00:29.615
The ticket has the required labels, symphony in the default workflow.

s2.3
00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:35.037
It is in an active state: Todo, In Progress or Rework. In Review and Merging are parked.

s2.4
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:40.305
A repo: label names the repository. Without one, the default repository applies.

s2.5
00:00:40.305 --> 00:00:43.988
Its blockers are terminal, no person holds it, and a slot is free.

s2.6
00:00:43.988 --> 00:00:50.206
A ticket with Symphony's labels is told once whether it is queued. If not, a comment says what is missing.

s3.1
00:00:50.556 --> 00:00:55.052
When a ticket changes, the tracker sends a signed webhook to the control API.

s3.2
00:00:55.052 --> 00:01:00.325
The API wakes the daemon at once. A safety poll also runs every 15 seconds.

s3.3
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:06.601
The daemon claims the ticket and takes a dispatch token. Only the first writer wins, so nothing starts twice.

s3.4
00:01:06.601 --> 00:01:08.967
Then it creates a runner Job for this attempt.

s3.5
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:13.304
In the mocked run, the session started 44 milliseconds after the ticket.

s4.1
00:01:13.654 --> 00:01:17.491
The runner is trusted. It keeps the tracker key and the GitHub App key.

s4.2
00:01:17.491 --> 00:01:21.944
It creates or reuses the ticket's workspace pod, with a volume for the session.

s4.3
00:01:21.944 --> 00:01:27.491
It mints a GitHub token for the ticket's repositories, for one hour at most, in a private file.

s4.4
00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:33.414
The model settings and MCP servers go into a private agent dir, made fresh for each attempt.

s4.5
00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:38.221
The pod has no ServiceAccount token, and it cannot reach the API or Redis.

s4.6
00:01:38.221 --> 00:01:42.420
The runner starts omp in the pod, through the Kubernetes API.

s5.1
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.804
WORKFLOW.md has the settings at the top and a prompt template below.

s5.2
00:01:46.804 --> 00:01:50.816
The template is strict Liquid, with two values: the issue and the attempt.

s5.3
00:01:50.816 --> 00:01:55.147
The default prompt asks for a ready pull request, and then a move to In Review.

s5.4
00:01:55.147 --> 00:02:00.644
The first step is to move the ticket to In Progress and post the Workpad, one comment edited in place.

s5.5
00:02:00.644 --> 00:02:05.534
On a retry, the last failure summary is added. Later turns get a short prompt.

s6.1
00:02:05.884 --> 00:02:11.268
An attempt is a series of turns, up to 20. Before each one, the runner reads the ticket state.

s6.2
00:02:11.268 --> 00:02:15.574
Every engine event goes to the session stream in Redis, and Tern shows it live.

s6.3
00:02:15.574 --> 00:02:19.633
The tracker tool runs in the runner, so the tracker key never enters the pod.

s6.4
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:24.364
People can steer, follow up or interrupt. The runner applies each message at once.

s6.5
00:02:24.364 --> 00:02:28.394
The agent opens a ready pull request and moves the ticket to In Review.

s6.6
00:02:28.394 --> 00:02:32.456
The daemon parks the record and posts a session report with the cost.

s7.1
00:02:32.806 --> 00:02:37.790
If the merger blocks the pull request, the ticket goes to Rework, and the agent runs again.

s7.2
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.296
The prompt adds a Rework section: fix only what the report marks as blocking.

s7.3
00:02:42.296 --> 00:02:47.857
The agent pushes to the same branch, marks the pull request ready, and moves the ticket to In Review.

s7.4
00:02:47.857 --> 00:02:50.134
The merger then reviews only the delta.

s7.5
00:02:50.134 --> 00:02:56.124
Meanwhile, a question on the pull request starts a short answer run. The ticket state does not change.

s8.1
00:02:56.474 --> 00:03:00.496
An attempt ends as done, continuation, blocked, failed or fenced.

s8.2
00:03:00.496 --> 00:03:05.275
A continuation runs the same attempt again, after a cooldown if nothing changed.

s8.3
00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:11.883
Blocked means the agent waits for a person. It called symphony_blocked, or three continuation turns changed nothing.

s8.4
00:03:11.883 --> 00:03:17.589
A failure starts a new attempt after a backoff: 10, 20, 40 seconds, and up to 5 minutes.

s8.5
00:03:17.589 --> 00:03:24.187
After 8 failures in a row, the retries stop. The ticket is blocked, with a report of what failed and who acts.

s8.6
00:03:24.187 --> 00:03:29.872
With triage on, the failure also opens an investigation ticket, where an agent looks for the cause.

s8.7
00:03:29.872 --> 00:03:34.697
If the agent goes silent for two windows, the daemon stops it and says so on the ticket.

s8.8
00:03:34.697 --> 00:03:38.639
A runner Job that ended without an outcome is requeued at the next tick.

s8.9
00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:42.641
The branch and the Workpad stay, so the next attempt continues the work.

s9.1
00:03:42.991 --> 00:03:48.220
When the agent needs a decision that is not its own, it calls symphony_ask, and names who decides.

s9.2
00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:54.063
The daemon posts the question on the ticket, mentioning that person, and in Slack, with one button per option.

s9.3
00:03:54.063 --> 00:03:57.915
That person and the ticket's assignee also get it as a direct message.

s9.4
00:03:57.915 --> 00:04:03.786
An answer on the ticket, the pull request, Slack, Tern or the CLI counts. The first one wins.

s9.5
00:04:03.786 --> 00:04:08.589
If nobody answers in 15 minutes, the agent goes on and records its assumption.

s9.6
00:04:08.589 --> 00:04:15.024
The question stays open. After 2 hours, a reminder goes out once, and a late answer still reaches the work.

s9.7
00:04:15.024 --> 00:04:18.374
A person can also hold the ticket, with symphony takeover.

s9.8
00:04:18.374 --> 00:04:23.080
The daemon stops the attempt and keeps the workspace. symphony release gives it back.

s10.1
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:28.673
An investigation asks for an answer, not a change. Its GitHub token can only read.

s10.2
00:04:28.673 --> 00:04:33.389
The agent writes a report on the ticket, files the follow-up work, and moves it to Done.

s10.3
00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:38.005
A large ticket becomes sub-issues, with the parent's labels and blocking relations.

s10.4
00:04:38.005 --> 00:04:44.280
Evidence, such as a test log, attaches to the ticket. On Linear, so does each run's session trace, redacted.

s10.5
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.984
A later run can read the ticket's files back, through two host tools.

s10.6
00:04:47.984 --> 00:04:54.578
Two repo: labels make a multi-repository ticket, with one token and one pull request per repository.

s10.7
00:04:54.578 --> 00:04:58.250
When its repositories change, the ticket gets a new workspace.

s11.1
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.901
Each tick first reconciles every tracked ticket with the tracker.

s11.2
00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:07.006
A ticket that left the active states is parked. It keeps its workspace and uses no slot.

s11.3
00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:11.755
When the ticket is Done, the daemon starts a retrospective, then deletes the workspace.

s11.4
00:05:11.755 --> 00:05:19.018
A ticket moved to Canceled or Duplicate stops everything. Its questions close, and the merger closes its pull requests.

s11.5
00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:24.850
The reflector reads the diff, the reviews, the CI and the Workpad, and proposes at most three lessons.

s11.6
00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:29.963
Code curates them into AGENTS.md, within a line budget. Unused lessons decay.

s11.7
00:05:29.963 --> 00:05:33.936
It opens a learnings pull request. A person must review and merge it.

s11.8
00:05:33.936 --> 00:05:39.524
In a public repository, the work never names the ticket, and no learnings pull request opens.

s11.9
00:05:39.524 --> 00:05:44.029
A nightly sweep starts the retrospectives that were missed in the last seven days.

s12.1
00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:49.337
On a kind cluster, a new ticket reached its runner Job in 7.7 seconds.

s12.2
00:05:49.337 --> 00:05:54.737
A steer reached the agent in 13 milliseconds, and the next attempt resumed the same session.

s12.3
00:05:54.737 --> 00:05:59.876
In the mocked run, the ticket reached In Review with a ready pull request in 22 seconds.

s12.4
00:05:59.876 --> 00:06:05.242
The investigation's push and pull request were refused, and the other scenarios passed.
